const KB=[[["sun", "our star"], "The Sun is a G-type main-sequence star (yellow dwarf) holding 99.8% of the Solar System’s mass. Its surface is about 5,500 °C and its core about 15 million °C, where hydrogen fuses into helium. Sunlight takes roughly 8 minutes 20 seconds to reach Earth. It is ~4.6 billion years old and will swell into a red giant in about 5 billion years."], [["mercury"], "Mercury is the smallest planet and the closest to the Sun. A year there lasts 88 Earth days, and temperatures swing from about −180 °C at night to 430 °C by day because it has almost no atmosphere."], [["venus"], "Venus is the hottest planet (~465 °C) thanks to a thick CO₂ atmosphere and runaway greenhouse effect. It rotates backwards, and a Venus day (243 Earth days) is longer than its year (225 days)."], [["earth"], "Earth is the only known planet with life: about 71% of its surface is water, it has one Moon, orbits the Sun in 365.25 days and is roughly 4.54 billion years old. Its magnetic field and atmosphere shield life from solar radiation."], [["moon", "lunar"], "The Moon is Earth’s only natural satellite, ~384,400 km away. It likely formed from a giant impact ~4.5 billion years ago and is tidally locked, so we always see the same face. Apollo 11 landed humans in 1969, and India’s Chandrayaan-3 landed near the lunar south pole in August 2023."], [["mars", "red planet"], "Mars is red because of iron oxide (rust) in its soil. It has the tallest volcano in the Solar System, Olympus Mons (~22 km), two small moons (Phobos and Deimos), and a day of about 24 h 37 min. Rovers like Curiosity and Perseverance explore it, and India’s Mangalyaan reached Mars orbit in 2014 on its first attempt."], [["jupiter"], "Jupiter is the largest planet — over 1,300 Earths could fit inside. It’s a gas giant with banded clouds, a storm called the Great Red Spot that has raged for centuries, and more than 90 known moons including Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto."], [["saturn", "rings"], "Saturn is famous for its rings of ice and rock. It is the least dense planet — it would float in water — and has over 140 known moons, including Titan, which has a thick atmosphere and methane lakes."], [["uranus"], "Uranus is an ice giant tilted ~98°, so it rolls around the Sun on its side. Methane gives it a cyan colour, and one orbit takes about 84 years."], [["neptune"], "Neptune is the farthest planet, a deep-blue ice giant with the fastest winds in the Solar System (up to ~2,000 km/h). It was found in 1846 through mathematical prediction, and one orbit takes about 165 years. Its big moon Triton orbits backwards."], [["pluto", "dwarf planet"], "Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006. NASA’s New Horizons flew past in 2015 and revealed a heart-shaped nitrogen-ice plain called Tombaugh Regio."], [["solar system", "planets"], "The Solar System formed ~4.6 billion years ago. It has eight planets — Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune — plus dwarf planets, an asteroid belt, the Kuiper Belt and the distant Oort Cloud."], [["black hole", "event horizon", "singularity"], "A black hole is a region where gravity is so strong that not even light can escape. Many form when a very massive star collapses; supermassive ones sit at galaxy centres. The boundary of no return is the event horizon. The first image of one (M87*) came in 2019, and our own galaxy’s Sagittarius A* was imaged in 2022."], [["hawking"], "Hawking radiation is a theoretical effect where black holes slowly lose energy and may eventually evaporate. It has not been observed directly."], [["supernova"], "A supernova is the explosion of a star. Type Ia happens when a white dwarf accretes matter and detonates; core-collapse supernovae end massive stars and leave a neutron star or black hole. The Crab Nebula is the remnant of the supernova seen in 1054 CE."], [["neutron star", "pulsar", "magnetar"], "A neutron star is the crushed core of a massive star — about 20 km wide, yet a teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tons. A pulsar is a spinning neutron star whose beams sweep past Earth like a lighthouse; a magnetar has an extremely powerful magnetic field."], [["milky way", "our galaxy"], "The Milky Way is a barred spiral galaxy ~100,000 light-years across with 100–400 billion stars. The Sun lies ~26,000 light-years from the centre, where the black hole Sagittarius A* (~4 million solar masses) sits."], [["galaxy", "galaxies", "andromeda"], "Galaxies are huge collections of stars, gas, dust and dark matter, and come as spirals, ellipticals and irregulars. The nearby Andromeda galaxy is ~2.5 million light-years away and will merge with the Milky Way in roughly 4–5 billion years."], [["nebula", "nebulae", "pillars of creation"], "Nebulae are clouds of gas and dust. Some are stellar nurseries where stars form (like the Orion Nebula and the Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula); others, like the Ring Nebula, are the glowing remains of dying stars."], [["star", "stars", "life cycle"], "Stars are born in nebulae, shine by fusing hydrogen, then age: Sun-like stars become red giants and then white dwarfs, while massive stars explode as supernovae and leave neutron stars or black holes."], [["white dwarf", "red giant"], "A red giant is a swollen, cooler star running out of core hydrogen. After shedding its outer layers, a Sun-like star leaves behind a white dwarf — an Earth-sized, extremely dense stellar ember."], [["comet"], "Comets are icy bodies that grow glowing tails when they approach the Sun; the tails always point away from it. Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years (next in 2061)."], [["asteroid", "meteor", "meteorite"], "Asteroids are rocky leftovers from planet formation, mostly in the belt between Mars and Jupiter. A meteoroid is a small piece of space rock, a meteor is the streak of light when it burns up, and a meteorite is a piece that reaches the ground."], [["big bang", "origin of the universe"], "The Big Bang is the model in which the universe expanded from an extremely hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago. Evidence includes the expansion of space and the cosmic microwave background."], [["dark matter", "dark energy"], "Ordinary matter makes up only ~5% of the universe. Dark matter (~27%) is unseen mass that holds galaxies together; dark energy (~68%) is the mysterious effect that drives the accelerating expansion."], [["age of the universe", "size of the universe", "observable universe"], "The universe is about 13.8 billion years old, and the observable part is roughly 93 billion light-years across."], [["light year", "light-year", "speed of light"], "A light-year is the distance light travels in a year — about 9.46 trillion km. Light moves at 299,792 km/s."], [["wormhole"], "A wormhole (Einstein–Rosen bridge) is a theoretical tunnel through spacetime. Nothing like it has been observed, and keeping one open would likely need exotic matter."], [["iss", "space station", "international space"], "The International Space Station orbits ~400 km above Earth at about 28,000 km/h, circling the planet roughly every 90 minutes. It has been continuously crewed since November 2000 and is a joint project of space agencies including NASA, Roscosmos, ESA, JAXA and CSA."], [["telescope", "jwst", "james webb", "hubble"], "Hubble (launched 1990) studies the universe mainly in visible and ultraviolet light. The James Webb Space Telescope (launched 25 Dec 2021) observes in infrared from the L2 point and sees some of the earliest galaxies."], [["exoplanet"], "Exoplanets orbit other stars. Thousands (well over 5,000) have been confirmed, from hot Jupiters to rocky worlds in habitable zones."], [["gravity"], "Gravity is the attraction between masses. Einstein’s general relativity describes it as the curvature of spacetime, which explains orbits, black holes and gravitational waves."], [["eclipse"], "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth; a lunar eclipse happens when Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon."], [["aurora", "northern lights"], "Auroras happen when charged particles from the Sun hit Earth’s upper atmosphere along magnetic field lines, making gases glow green, red and purple."], [["isro", "chandrayaan", "mangalyaan", "aditya", "gaganyaan"], "ISRO highlights: Chandrayaan-3 landed near the Moon’s south pole in August 2023; Mangalyaan (2014) made India the first country to reach Mars orbit on its first attempt; Aditya-L1 (2023) studies the Sun; Gaganyaan is India’s crewed spaceflight programme. Check ISRO’s site for the latest status."], [["astronaut", "first human", "gagarin", "armstrong", "rakesh sharma"], "Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space in 1961, Neil Armstrong the first on the Moon in 1969, and Rakesh Sharma the first Indian in space in 1984."], [["rocket", "escape velocity"], "Rockets work by Newton’s third law: pushing exhaust down pushes the rocket up. To leave Earth you need about 11.2 km/s (escape velocity), and ~7.8 km/s to stay in low Earth orbit."], [["voyager"], "Voyager 1 and 2 launched in 1977. Voyager 1 entered interstellar space in 2012 and is the farthest human-made object."], [["habitable", "life"], "The habitable zone is the orbital region where liquid water could exist on a planet’s surface. Life elsewhere is unproven; Mars, Europa and Enceladus are prime places to look."], [["quasar", "gamma"], "A quasar is an extremely bright galactic nucleus powered by a feeding supermassive black hole. Gamma-ray bursts are the most powerful explosions known, linked to dying massive stars and neutron-star mergers."], [["relativity", "time dilation"], "Einstein’s relativity says time runs slower near strong gravity and at high speeds. GPS satellites must correct for this to stay accurate."], [["microwave background", "cmb"], "The cosmic microwave background is the faint afterglow of the early universe, released about 380,000 years after the Big Bang."]];
